title op6: both links renamed, the other side quiet

# The defect op6-set-succession.md exists to fix, in the smallest
# tree that shows it.  Settled by the lead 2026-08-31, so the gold
# below is the SETTLED answer and not what the engine does today --
# the engine is behind, and this fixture is what says so.
#
# Pool 10 holds two names, x and y.  Off-of renames both.  Onto does
# nothing whatever.
#
# OLD BEHAVIOUR.  Definition 9.3 pairs a gained name with a lost one
# only when there is exactly one of each; two lost and two gained
# fell to the degrade branch, the gains became attachments, and with
# no opposition from onto the pool MATERIALIZED.  A hard-linked pair
# quietly became a different object -- onto-side links and open
# handles dropped -- because somebody renamed two files.  Nothing in
# the report said so, which is why "at least it fails loudly" was not
# true.
#
# SETTLED BEHAVIOUR.  Succession is set-valued: the pool lost {x, y}
# and gained {aa, bb}, with no antecedent labels and no degrade.
# Realization runs two passes.  Pass one is name-driven and resolves
# nothing here, since neither aa nor bb exists in onto.  Pass two
# routes by the delta's LOST set: x and y both lie in onto's pool 10,
# nothing else has claimed it, and the type matches -- so pool 10 is
# the answer and the dnode is kept.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed, /shared is green
#   deltas:          pool 10 lost {x, y}, gained {aa, bb}
#   decisions:       one output pool holding aa and bb, reusing
#                    onto's dnode 10-100.  No materialize, no unlink
#   conflicts:       none.  Nothing opposed anything
#   emission:        two renames inside /shared's scope; the pool is
#                    the same object throughout, so no link or
#                    unlink appears
#
# devcheck/treecases/ok-wholesale-rename.tree is this shape three
# times over with pools that keep a name; this one keeps none, which
# is what removes the name-driven route and forces the fallback.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: bb

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: bb
